    <heading level="1">Extensions</heading>
    <exercise number="45" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>In the expansion of $${(5x + 3y)}^{n},$$ each term has the form $$\begin{pmatrix} n \\ k \end{pmatrix}a^{n-k}b^{k}$$ , where $$k$$ successively takes on the value $$0,1,2,..., n.$$ If $$\begin{pmatrix} n \\ k \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix},$$ what is the corresponding term?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$590,625 x 5 y 2 590,625 x 5 y 2$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="46" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>In the expansion of $$\left( {a + b} \right)^{n},$$ the coefficient of $$a^{n - k}b^{k}$$ is the same as the coefficient of which other term?</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="47" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Consider the expansion of $${(x + b)}^{40}.$$ What is the exponent of $$b$$ in the $$k \text{th}$$ term?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$k-1 k-1$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="48" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find $$\begin{pmatrix} n \\ {k - 1} \end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix} n \\ k \end{pmatrix}$$ and write the answer as a binomial coefficient in the form $$\begin{pmatrix} n \\ k \end{pmatrix}.$$ Prove it. Hint: Use the fact that, for any integer $$p,$$ such that $$p \geq 1, p! = p(p - 1)!.$$</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="49" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Which expression cannot be expanded using the Binomial Theorem? Explain.</paragraph>
        <list type="unordered">
          <item>$$(x^{2} - 2x + 1)$$</item>
          <item>$${(\sqrt{a} + 4\sqrt{a} - 5)}^{8}$$</item>
          <item>$${(x^{3} + 2y^{2} - z)}^{5}$$</item>
          <item>$${(3x^{2} - \sqrt{2y^{3}})}^{12}$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>The expression $$( x 3 +2 y 2 -z) 5 ( x 3 +2 y 2 -z) 5$$ cannot be expanded using the Binomial Theorem because it cannot be rewritten as a binomial.</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
